ns so kind of  the flip side we can talk about how to  raise money but now how to invest first  one is while investing what are some of  the important things to look for in an  annual report okay well there's just a  few numbers that really matter okay so  an annual report the number one thing  we're looking for as an investor is cash  flow and amazingly it's not in the  annual report you have to you have to do  the math so for me I go directly to and  we're talking about the numbers here I  go directly to the cash flow page  there's a bold line called operating  cash flow I look at that number  then I look right below at about three  lines down there's one that says  purchase of property and equipment which  is your capital expenditures subtract  that from your operating cash flow and  you've got free cash I want to see that  free cash number is what I'm looking for  an investment so that's the first thing  second thing as soon as I see there's a  there's free cash coming through this  company  the next thing I'm looking for is what's  the moat I want to see that in the 10k  typically in the competition section  that says you know here's how we're  competing against other companies and  there should be a statement in there  from the CEO that says we have a an  advantage in this industry because we do  ABC so if you're looking at Apple  Computer it's going to say our our  expertise and what gives us an advantage  in this industry is that we build an  ecosystem that's comprised of software  hardware network and so on and they're  basically saying this is how we protect  ourselves from people coming in and  taking away our business and why because  of that you can estimate what the future  looks like  because if you can't do that who knows  what this thing is worth right so if you  want to invest in companies you want to  invest in companies that have a lot of  cash flow and that you can look at have  a big moat so that you can project  they'll be worth more in ten years than  they are today those are the two things  we're digging for right off the bat not  it here's here's the question feel it  officially came in I'm an entrepreneur  should I go to college what do you think  Phil I'm an entrepreneur should I go to  college I don't think that that is a is  a binary decision I think you just got  to decide what am I trying to do and  what can I learn when I go to college so  I'll tell you what what I did I went  back to college after I was about 23 or  24 years old and I studied philosophy  and that was one of the best things I  ever did and it was completely useless  in terms of you know being an  entrepreneur or being an investor I had  to go learn all that stuff later or at  the same time actually and you know it  just depends on what you're gonna study  and what you want to do with college you  know I I can't give you that answer is  it necessary no it's not necessary  got it the next one is an investing  question but can relate to  entrepreneurship too how do I cope after  making a wrong investment which can also  be an entrepreneurship you have a  failure so how'd it can people cope with  making those mistakes okay so the first  thing is to just recognize the mistake  as quickly as possible and rectify it  what we do when we invest in a company  as we look at us we create what's called  a story and if that story changes then  like for example we think the story  basically is this is gonna great company  and we're buying it a really great price  great ok then the story changes and it's  clearly not what we thought it was we're  gonna exit that investment without  looking backwards  we're just gonna say we're out of here  so the first thing you got to understand  about making a mistake is we all make  them it's sort of inevitable you're  gonna have them what you want to do is  you don't want to compound that mistake  with false hope and just continue to  invest or continue to work on building  something that isn't gonna work 